    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.canonical_labeling - EdgeCase\",\n    \"isPartOf\": {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.canonical_labeling.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.canonical_labeling()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: >-\n    Number the nodes so that isomorphic graphs, renumbered, become identical.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.canonical_labeling()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: >-\n    Number the nodes so that isomorphic graphs, renumbered, become identical.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.canonical_labeling()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: >-\n  Number the nodes so that isomorphic graphs, renumbered, become identical.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nNumber the nodes so that isomorphic graphs, renumbered, become identical.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L27548-L27766){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.canonical_labeling(\n    node_attr=None,\n    edge_attr=None,\n)\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Canonical labeling](../assets/graphs/canonical-labeling.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"Two drawings of a four-node path: g is the straight path A, B, C, D, and h is the path y, w, x, z drawn as a Z. Each node carries its canonical number in a badge. The ends get 0 and 1 (A and D, y and z, teal) and the middle nodes get 2 and 3 (B and C, w and x, accent). Rewritten with these numbers, both graphs have the edges 0–2, 1–3 and 2–3, so the card shows the same canonical form for each, and equal is True.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nA canonical labeling gives every node a number from 0 to $n - 1$ in a way that depends\nonly on the graph's structure: rewrite two graphs with their canonical numbers in place\nof their nodes, and the results are equal exactly when the graphs are isomorphic. The\nnumbers do not depend on labels, IDs or the order in which nodes and edges were added,\nand nodes the graph cannot tell apart (symmetric ones) are numbered consistently.\n\nCanonical labelings are used to:\n\n- deduplicate a large collection of graphs (molecules, circuits, patterns) by hashing a\n  canonical form, instead of testing every pair with `is_isomorphic()`\n- store graphs under a canonical key, or compare a graph to a catalog\n- number the atoms of a molecule reproducibly, as in chemical registry systems\n:::\n\n## Parameters {.doc-parameters}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code>[node_attr]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str | list[str] | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Optional node attribute name, or list of names, whose values the numbering must\n    respect: an isomorphism then has to map nodes onto nodes with equal values (a missing\n    attribute counts as `None`).\n\n<code>[edge_attr]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str | list[str] | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Optional edge attribute name, or list of names, to respect in the same way for edges.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [dict[int, int]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   For every node ID, in ID order, its canonical number.\n:::\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~is_isomorphic`) \n\n:   Decide isomorphism of two graphs directly.\n\n[](`~automorphism_cosets`) \n\n:   The graph's symmetries.\n\n[](`~weisfeiler_lehman_graph_hash`) \n\n:   A fast hash that isomorphic graphs share (but some non-isomorphic graphs share it too).\n\n## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}\n\nThe labeling comes from an individualization-refinement search, as in nauty (McKay and\nPiperno, 2014). Color refinement (the 1-dimensional Weisfeiler-Leman algorithm) first\nsplits the nodes into the coarsest equitable partition of the attribute colors. Each\nnode of the search tree then picks the first smallest non-singleton cell, gives each of\nits members in turn a cell of its own, and refines again; the leaves are discrete\npartitions, that is, numberings. Every tree node carries an invariant (a hash of the\nrefinement trace), and the canonical leaf is the one with the largest sequence of\ninvariants on its path, ties broken by the largest graph rewritten under the numbering.\nTwo leaves with the same path and the same rewritten graph differ by an automorphism;\neach one found prunes the search (the subtrees of nodes in the same orbit are equivalent,\nand the search jumps back to the leaves' common ancestor), and cells of twins (nodes with\nthe same neighbors) are not branched on at all. A tree node whose partition is uniform\n(every permutation within cells is a symmetry) also counts as a leaf. Edge direction,\nparallel edges (their number and attributes), and self-loops all count.\n\nThis follows NetworkX pull request #8953 (not yet merged), with some differences. That\nversion compares numberings by their sorted undirected endpoint pairs, so it ignores\nedge direction (and, without an `edge_attr` option, edge attributes); here both count.\nIt also explores the search tree level by level, deduplicating with isomorphism tests,\nwhich grows quickly with the graph's symmetries, where this search prunes with the\nautomorphisms it finds. The numbers themselves depend on the details of the search, so\nthey differ from that version's (and from nauty's): only the property that isomorphic\ngraphs get identical canonical forms is guaranteed, and the numbers are stable from\none call to the next.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nTwo drawings of the same path, with different labels and edges added in a different\norder, get canonical numberings that turn both into the same graph:\n\n::: {#4af78c48 .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\ng = ec.Graph.from_edges([(\"A\", \"B\"), (\"B\", \"C\"), (\"C\", \"D\")])\nh = ec.Graph.from_edges([(\"y\", \"w\"), (\"x\", \"z\"), (\"w\", \"x\")])\n\n\ndef canonical_form(graph, **kwargs):\n    number = graph.canonical_labeling(**kwargs)\n    return sorted(\n        tuple(sorted((number[e.source], number[e.target])))\n        for e in map(graph.get_edge, graph.get_edge_ids(limit=None))\n    )\n\n\ncanonical_form(g), canonical_form(g) == canonical_form(h)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n([(0, 2), (1, 3), (2, 3)], True)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nThe numbering, by label: the two ends of the path, and the two middle nodes, are\nnumbered alike in both graphs.\n\n::: {#a2e582f7 .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\ndef by_label(graph):\n    return {\n        graph.get_label_from_id(n): k\n        for n, k in graph.canonical_labeling().items()\n    }\n\n\nby_label(g), by_label(h)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```\n({'A': 0, 'B': 2, 'C': 3, 'D': 1}, {'y': 0, 'w': 2, 'x': 3, 'z': 1})\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nA six-node cycle and two triangles have the same degrees, and color refinement cannot\nseparate them, but their canonical forms differ:\n\n::: {#45c4c100 .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nhexagon = ec.Graph().add_cycle_graph(6)\ntwo_triangles = ec.Graph().add_cycle_graph(3).add_cycle_graph(3)\n\ncanonical_form(hexagon) == canonical_form(two_triangles)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```\nFalse\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### Recognizing a molecule\n\nChemists number the ring atoms of caffeine 1 to 9, by the scheme for purines.\n`molecules()[\"caffeine\"]` numbers the atoms of each element in turn instead. With the\nelements taken into account, both get the same canonical form, so they're the same\nmolecule:\n\n::: {#406285d3 .cell execution_count=4}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\ncaffeine = ec.molecules()[\"caffeine\"]\npurine = ec.Graph.from_edges(\n    [\n        (\"N1\", \"C2\"), (\"C2\", \"N3\"), (\"N3\", \"C4\"), (\"C4\", \"C5\"),\n        (\"C5\", \"C6\"), (\"C6\", \"N1\"), (\"C4\", \"N9\"), (\"N9\", \"C8\"),\n        (\"C8\", \"N7\"), (\"N7\", \"C5\"), (\"C2\", \"O10\"), (\"C6\", \"O11\"),\n        (\"N1\", \"C12\"), (\"N3\", \"C13\"), (\"N7\", \"C14\"),\n    ]\n)\npurine.set_node_attrs(\n    \"element\", {atom: atom[0] for atom in purine.get_node_df()[\"label\"]}\n)\n\n(\n    canonical_form(caffeine, node_attr=\"element\")\n    == canonical_form(purine, node_attr=\"element\")\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=4}\n```\nTrue\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nLabeled with their canonical numbers, matching atoms carry the same number in both\ndrawings, such as `3` for the methyl carbon that is C1 in one and C14 in the other:\n\n::: {layout-ncol=\"2\"}\n\n::: {#9ed1e159 .cell execution_count=5}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nfor molecule in [caffeine, purine]:\n    molecule.set_node_attrs(\n        \"canonical\", molecule.canonical_labeling(node_attr=\"element\")\n    )\n\ncaffeine.plot(\n    color_by=\"element\", label_by=\"canonical\", legend=False, seed=1,\n    width=360, height=360,\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=5}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){.ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}}:is(html.quarto-light, body.quarto-light, html[data-bs-theme=\"light\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"true\"], body.vscode-light, body.vscode-high-contrast-light, html[theme=\"light\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}:is(html.quarto-dark, body.quarto-dark, html[data-bs-theme=\"dark\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"false\"], body.vscode-dark, body.vscode-high-contrast:not(.vscode-high-contrast-light), html[theme=\"dark\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}</style><div class=\"ec-plot\" style=\"position:relative;width:fit-content;max-width:100%;margin:1em auto\">\n<style>.ec-plot > svg { display:block; max-width:100%; height:auto; }</style>\n<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"360\" height=\"360\" viewBox=\"5.6 15.6 348.9 348.9\" style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, 'Helvetica Neue', Arial, sans-serif;\">\n<rect x=\"5.6\" y=\"15.6\" width=\"348.9\" height=\